        title="Ekman transport: why the sea moves sideways to the wind",
        kicker="The wind pushes the sea, but the sea moves at a right angle to it.",
        simple=[
            "When the wind blows across the sea, you would expect the water to move the same way. It does not. The surface water moves off at an angle, about 45° to the right of the wind in the Northern Hemisphere and 45° to the left in the Southern. Each layer beneath is dragged by the one above and turned a bit further. The result is a spiral, and averaged over the top 50 to 100 metres the water moves at **90° to the wind**.",
            "The Norwegian explorer Fridtjof Nansen noticed it in the 1890s: during his Arctic expedition he saw that sea ice drifted 20 to 40° to the right of the wind. He put the puzzle to the physicist Vilhelm Bjerknes, whose student Vagn Walfrid Ekman worked out the answer between 1902 and 1905. It is still taught today.",
            "Ekman transport matters because it causes upwelling and downwelling. Winds blowing along a coast push water offshore, and cold water rises. Winds that go round a high-pressure system push surface water together in the middle of an ocean gyre and make it sink. This links the winds above to the deep currents below.",
        ],
        detail=[
            ("The depth of the spiral", "The Ekman layer is about 40 to 100 metres deep, depending on the wind and the latitude. At the bottom of the layer the current is opposite to the surface current and weaker by a factor of about 23 (e^{π})."),
            ("Why it happens", "A parcel of water pushed by the wind is deflected by the Coriolis effect. This slows it down; each layer below feels friction from the layer above and the Coriolis effect turns it again. Only when the Coriolis force on the whole layer balances the wind stress does the motion settle, and the net flow is at 90°."),
            ("Pumping", "If the wind is such that the Ekman transport varies from place to place, water must come up or go down to make up the difference. In the centre of a subtropical gyre the transport converges, water sinks (downwelling or Ekman pumping); in subpolar gyres and along the equator it diverges, and water rises."),
            ("Real oceans", "The exact spiral is hard to see in the sea, because waves, storms and changing winds disturb it, but the net transport at 90° is very well established and is used to calculate how much water the winds move in satellite-based ocean models."),
        ],
        expert=[
            ("Ekman layer equations", "With constant eddy viscosity K the steady balance f k × u = K ∂²u/∂z² has the solution\nEQ: u + iv = (√2 τ / (ρ f D)) e^{z/D} e^{i(z/D − π/4)},   D = √(2K/|f|)\nso the surface current is 45° to the right of the wind in the Northern Hemisphere. The depth-integrated transport is M = τ × k̂ / (ρ f), at 90° to the wind and independent of K."),
            ("Ekman pumping", "The vertical velocity at the base of the layer is\nEQ: w_E = (1/ρ₀) k̂ · ∇ × (τ/f)\nwhich gives downwelling of about 30 to 50 m per year in the centre of the subtropical gyres."),
        ],
