            ("Measuring SST", "SST was once measured with a bucket on a rope, then in the engine intake of ships, and since the 1980s from buoys and satellites. Datasets such as ERSST and OISST combine all of them and correct for the changes in method."),
            ("The sea-surface temperature map", "The map is NOAA's Optimum Interpolation SST (OISST) averaged over 1991–2020. The 'Sea warming since 1901' layer shows the trend derived from ERSST v5. The North Atlantic south of Greenland and parts of the Southern Ocean have warmed little or even cooled, while the Indian Ocean, the western boundary currents (the Gulf Stream, Kuroshio and Agulhas regions) and the Arctic margins have warmed fastest."),
            ("Marine heatwaves", "Periods of unusually warm water that last for five days or more are called marine heatwaves. The number of marine heatwave days a year rose by about 50% between 1925–1954 and 1987–2016, and heatwaves have killed coral reefs, kelp forests and fish."),
