            ("Thinner and younger", "The Arctic ice that survives several summers has declined sharply. Ice more than four years old made up about a third of the March ice cover in 1985 and barely 1% in 2019 (NOAA Arctic Report Card); a younger pack is thinner and easier to melt or break."),
            ("Why it matters", "Ice reflects sunlight, insulates the sea from the cold air, and when it forms leaves salty water that sinks and drives the deep circulation. Less ice means more coastal erosion, fresh waves, changing storm tracks and a harder life for polar bears and seals, but also new shipping routes and more access to resources."),
